	3. Java build packaging: There are 3 subsections here 
		3.1 Development stages
			I. Java source code is developed in an editor
			II. This source code is transformed into bytecode by the compiler
			III. Bytecode is then run in the java virtual machine which is a virtual environment that in turn transforms the bytecode into machine readable format
			IV. Note: This makes java cross-platform, if the JVM can be installed on the os then it could execute the code written in java language.
		3.2 Manual process
			I. Source code is developed. Let filename be 'MyClass.java'
			II. Sourced code is compiled(transformed into bytecode, JVM readable format). 
				- Command line to compile: "javac MyClass.java" and the output file is 'MyClass'
			III. Compiled code is run
				- Command line to run java bytecode: "java MyClass"
			IV. There exist many java files in a complex programming project and since they work together they need to be bundled/packaged all at once with an utility called 'jar'.
				- This command is used like tar and the first java file should be the main file.
				- Command line to package: 'jar cf MyApp.jar MyClass.class Service1.class Service2.class ...'
			V. To document the source code
				- Command line: "javadoc -d MyClass.java"
			VI. Build process overview
				- javac MyClass.java
				- java -jar MyApp.jar
				- javadoc -d 'MyClass.java'	
		3.3 Automated process
			I. As always we can automate the carefully defined set of instructions.
			II. Maven, gradle and ant programs are used in java ecosystem to automate the build process.
